@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Administration";

    var message = TempData["AdminMessage"] as string;
}

<h1>Administration</h1>

@if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(message))
{
    @* Deleting redirects here. An irreversible action that lands on a silent page reads
       as though nothing happened. *@
    <div class="sc-notice" role="status"><p>@message</p></div>
}

<h2 class="h5">Available</h2>
<ul>
    <li><a asp-action="Periods">Periods</a></li>
</ul>

@*
    Planned, not linked. These used to be plain links to pages that answered "Not built yet."
    -- a menu entry that leads nowhere is worse than no entry, because it costs a click to
    find out. They are listed so the plan stays visible; the pages themselves say what they
    will do and who owns them (see Views/Shared/_NotBuiltYet.cshtml).
*@
<h2 class="h5">Planned</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Users and roles <span class="sc-badge sc-badge--muted">Not built yet</span></li>
    <li>Teams and coaches <span class="sc-badge sc-badge--muted">Not built yet</span></li>
    <li>Audit log view <span class="sc-badge sc-badge--muted">Not built yet</span></li>
</ul>

@*
    Built, but reached per player rather than from a list. Both take a player id in the URL,
    and the page that would let an admin pick one is the Users work above. Listed here so the
    two GDPR routes are not invisible until then.
*@
<h2 class="h5">Per player, by id</h2>
<ul>
    <li>
        Data access request &mdash; <span class="sc-code">/Admin/Export/{id}</span>.
        Downloads everything held about one player as JSON.
    </li>
    <li>
        Delete a player &mdash; <span class="sc-code">/Admin/Delete/{id}</span>.
        Shows what the deletion takes and asks for the player code before it runs.
    </li>
</ul>

<p class="text-muted">
    Administrators see every player. Lookups of an individual player's answers are written
    to the audit log (PlayerAccessEvent), which is also what stands in for the consent check
    coaches used to need. A deletion is logged separately, in PlayerDeletionEvent, because
    the audit log rows cascade away with the player they describe.
</p>
